<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AddRazorSupportForMvc>true</AddRazorSupportForMvc>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Title>uBookIt — front end</Title>
    <!--
      "Views are overridable per site" was here, and it was false. These views are compiled
      into this assembly without source checksums, so a file placed at the same path in a
      consuming site is never consulted — the trap a site author falls into first, which
      openspec/specs/packaging/spec.md has an explicit SHALL NOT about and which
      docs/booking-page.md documents as a route that does not work. The supported route is a
      theme. Do not reintroduce the shorter phrasing.
    -->
    <Description>What a visitor actually sees: the uBookIt booking flow's Razor views and view components, the booking page document type, the delivery API, and a stylesheet a site can restyle or replace. The markup itself is replaced by supplying a theme. Part of uBookIt, a booking system for Umbraco; install the UBookIt package rather than this one.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Umbraco.Cms.Api.Common" />
    <PackageReference Include="Umbraco.Cms.Web.Website" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package manifest ImportBookingPageSchema installs from. It is resolved by
    resource name, derived from this path — so the folder and the migration's
    namespace must agree.

    A mismatch is NOT silent: the resource lookup throws, the plan is constructed
    during boot, and the site fails to start with BootFailedException and 500s every
    request. PackagingTests asserts the coupling anyway, because a failing test names
    the cause in a second and a failed boot costs a deploy to diagnose.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Packaging\package.xml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="UBookIt.Tests" />

    <!--
      The resolution guard lives in the rendering suite, because that is the only
      project whose dependency closure has no runtime compilation in it. It calls
      the package's own registration step so that what it measures is the package's
      behaviour rather than a chain the test assembled to suit itself.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="UBookIt.Tests.Rendering" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\UBookIt.Core\UBookIt.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
